.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt CHPASS 1
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm chpass ,
.Nm chfn ,
.Nm chsh
.Nd change a user's full name or login shell
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm chpass
.Op Fl f Ar "full name"
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Ar user
.Nm chfn
.Op Fl f Ar "full name"
.Op Ar user
.Nm chsh
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These change the two fields of a DirectoryServices account that an ordinary
user is allowed to change: the full name and the login shell.
With no options the record is printed and nothing is altered.
.Pp
Run with no
.Ar user ,
they act on your own account.
Only root may name somebody else.
.Pp
.Sy The three names are not interchangeable .
.Nm chfn
changes the full name and refuses
.Fl s .
.Nm chsh
changes the shell and refuses
.Fl f .
Only
.Nm chpass
does both.
This differs from other BSDs, where all three are one command under different
names and any of them will edit anything: a command called
.Nm chsh
that can quietly rewrite your full name is a trap, and the restriction costs
nothing.
.Ss What cannot be changed
Everything else in the record belongs to the administrator.
There is no uid, gid, home directory or login class to edit here, because the
property lists hold no such fields for a regular account: the home is computed
from the name and there are no classes.
.Xr pw 8
is the tool for the rest.
.Pp
There is no editor session either.
Two fields do not need one, and dropping somebody into
.Ev EDITOR
over a whole record invites them to change a uid and then wonder why nothing
happened.
.Ss Proving who you are
A user editing their own record is asked for their password first.
This is a deliberate addition.
The record is owned by root and these commands are setuid, so the usual
protection of file ownership proves nothing about who is at the keyboard, and
a moment at an unlocked terminal would otherwise be enough to point somebody's
login shell at a program of the attacker's choosing.
Root is not asked.
.Ss Shells
A shell must be an absolute path.
For an ordinary user it must also be listed in
.Xr shells 5 ,
and one that is not is refused.
Root gets a warning and the change is made, because an administrator
installing a shell outside
.Pa /etc/shells
generally knows what they are doing, while a user pointing their login at an
arbitrary binary does not get to decide that.
.Pp
A shell that is not executable is a warning in both cases, not a refusal: the
file may simply not be installed yet.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-f full name"
.It Fl f Ar "full name"
Set the full name.
It may not contain a colon.
.It Fl s Ar shell
Set the login shell.
.El
.Sh NIS
.Nm ypchpass ,
.Nm ypchfn
and
.Nm ypchsh
exist and do nothing but report that NIS is not supported on NextBSD.
They are shipped so that a script or a habit that reaches for them gets an
answer, rather than a missing-command error about a name that exists
everywhere else.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
present when this machine is a directory client, in which case these commands
refuse: the record belongs to the server
.It Pa /etc/shells
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std chpass chfn chsh
Printing a record without changing it is a success.
.Sh EXAMPLES
See your own record:
.Pp
.Dl $ chpass
.Pp
Change your shell:
.Pp
.Dl $ chsh -s /bin/sh
.Pp
Change somebody else's full name:
.Pp
.Dl # chfn -f "Joe Maloney" joe
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr shells 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8 ,
.Xr vipw 8
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for NextBSD, to edit the DirectoryServices property lists.
It keeps the familiar names and drops the editor session and the fields that
this system does not store.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
